{"answer":"The evidence did not separate the two explanations. It was not enough to say whether the higher average delivery time came from a broad shift across most orders or from a small tail of very late orders.

Both tested claims stayed unresolved. The broad-shift explanation had 3 supporting evidence items and no refuting evidence, and the tail-of-late-orders explanation also had 3 supporting evidence items and no refuting evidence. Neither claim was supported strongly enough to stand on its own.

The run used a distribution summary, a dataset profile, and an outlier check on the 96-row operational_delivery dataset, then stopped because there was insufficient evidence. What remained open was whether the increase was spread across the distribution or concentrated in a few extreme delays, and whether the median or typical delivery time had moved rather than only the upper tail."}

